                <!-- Authority Documents -->
                <section class="content-section" id="authority-docs">
                    <h2>Authority Documents</h2>
                    <p>
                        Authority Documents represent <strong>external regulations, standards, and frameworks</strong> 
                        that your organization must comply with. These are the starting point of the compliance hierarchy.
                    </p>

                    <div class="data-table-wrapper">
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Table</th>
                                    <th>Key Fields</th>
                                    <th>Notes</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>sn_compliance_authority_document</code></td>
                                    <td>name, description, version, state, owner</td>
                                    <td>Extends <code>sn_grc_document</code>. Contains metadata about the regulation.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>sn_compliance_citation</code></td>
                                    <td>name, authority_document, reference, description</td>
                                    <td>Specific sections/clauses within an authority document.</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <p>
                        Common authority documents include NIST Cybersecurity Framework, ISO 27001, SOC 2, 
                        PCI-DSS, GDPR, HIPAA, and SOX. ServiceNow provides pre-built content packs for many 
                        of these frameworks that include the authority document, citations, and suggested 
                        control mappings.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <!-- Policies -->
                <section class="content-section" id="policies">
                    <h2>Policies</h2>
                    <p>
                        Policies are <strong>internal organizational documents</strong> that translate external 
                        regulatory requirements into actionable organizational rules. They bridge the gap between 
                        "what the law requires" and "what we do."
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Policies go through their own lifecycle:
                    </p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Draft:</strong> Policy is being written or updated</li>
                        <li><strong>Review:</strong> Policy is undergoing review by stakeholders</li>
                        <li><strong>Published:</strong> Policy is active and enforceable</li>
                        <li><strong>Retired:</strong> Policy is no longer applicable</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>
                        Each policy can be linked to one or more authority documents, establishing the regulatory 
                        basis for internal rules. Policies then contain <strong>Control Objectives (Policy Statements)</strong> 
                        that define specific, measurable requirements.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <!-- Controls -->
                <section class="content-section" id="controls">
                    <h2>Controls</h2>
                    <p>
                        Controls are the <strong>specific implementations</strong> of control objectives, assigned 
                        to specific entities. A single control objective can generate many controls — one for each 
                        entity that the objective applies to.
                    </p>

                    <h3>How Controls Are Generated</h3>
                    <p>
                        Controls can be created through the <strong>Content Reference</strong> mechanism. When you 
                        associate a Content Reference with both a control objective and an entity type, IRM 
                        automatically generates control records for each matching entity. This is called 
                        <strong>control scoping</strong>.
                    </p>

                    <div class="info-card info-card--tip">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Key Concept</div>
                        <p>
                            The relationship is: <strong>Control Objective</strong> (what must be achieved) → 
                            <strong>Control</strong> (specific implementation for a specific entity). One objective 
                            can produce hundreds of controls across your entity landscape.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <h3>Control States</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Draft:</strong> Control created but not yet active</li>
                        <li><strong>Review:</strong> Under review by control owner</li>
                        <li><strong>Attest:</strong> Owner attesting to control effectiveness</li>
                        <li><strong>Monitor:</strong> Active and being continuously monitored via indicators</li>
                        <li><strong>Not Applicable:</strong> Control doesn't apply to the entity</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>

                <!-- Indicators -->
                <section class="content-section" id="indicators">
                    <h2>Indicators</h2>
                    <p>
                        <strong>Indicators</strong> are the automated or manual tests that verify whether a control 
                        is actually working. They produce <strong>Pass/Fail results</strong> that determine the 
                        control's compliance status.
                    </p>

                    <div class="tabs-container">
                        <div class="tabs-header">
                            <button class="tab-btn active" data-tab="tab-manual">Manual Indicators</button>
                            <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="tab-automated">Automated Indicators</button>
                            <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="tab-scripted">Scripted Indicators</button>
                        </div>

                        <div class="tab-panel active" id="tab-manual">
                            <h3>Manual Indicators</h3>
                            <p>
                                Manual indicators generate <strong>attestation tasks</strong> that are assigned to 
                                control owners or designated assessors on a scheduled basis. The assignee manually 
                                verifies the control and records the result.
                            </p>
                            <p><strong>Use when:</strong> The control requires human judgment to verify (e.g., "Are security 
                            awareness training records current?" or "Has the disaster recovery plan been tested this quarter?").</p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-automated">
                            <h3>Automated Indicators</h3>
                            <p>
                                Automated indicators use <strong>declarative conditions</strong> or <strong>metric definitions</strong> 
                                to query ServiceNow data and determine Pass/Fail without human intervention. They run 
                                on a configurable schedule.
                            </p>
                            <p><strong>Use when:</strong> The control can be verified by querying system data (e.g., "Are all 
                            critical servers patched within 30 days?" — query the CMDB and patch management data).</p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-scripted">
                            <h3>Scripted Indicators</h3>
                            <p>
                                Scripted indicators use <strong>server-side JavaScript</strong> to implement complex 
                                verification logic. They're the most flexible option but require development effort.
                            </p>

                            <div class="code-block">
                                <div class="code-header">
                                    <span class="code-lang">Example — Scripted Indicator</span>
                                    <button class="code-copy">Copy</button>
                                </div>
                                <div class="code-body">
<pre><code>// Scripted Indicator: Check if all servers have 
// had a vulnerability scan in the last 30 days
(function(control, indicator) {
    var entity = control.profile;
    var ci = entity.applies_to;
    
    if (!ci) {
        return 'not_applicable';
    }
    
    var thirtyDaysAgo = gs.daysAgoStart(30);
    var gr = new GlideRecord('sn_vul_vulnerability');
    gr.addQuery('cmdb_ci', ci);
    gr.addQuery('last_scan_date', '>=', thirtyDaysAgo);
    gr.query();
    
    if (gr.hasNext()) {
        return 'pass'; // Scan found within 30 days
    } else {
        return 'fail'; // No recent scan
    }
})(control, indicator);</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <h3>Indicator Tables</h3>
                    <div class="data-table-wrapper">
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Table</th>
                                    <th>Purpose</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>sn_grc_indicator_template</code></td>
                                    <td>Reusable templates that define indicator logic and configuration</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>sn_grc_indicator</code></td>
                                    <td>Individual indicator instances linked to specific controls/entities</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>sn_grc_indicator_result</code></td>
                                    <td>Individual Pass/Fail results from each indicator run</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>sn_grc_indicator_supporting_data</code></td>
                                    <td>Evidence/data collected during indicator execution</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- Issues -->
                <section class="content-section" id="issues">
                    <h2>Issues & Remediation</h2>
                    <p>
                        When an indicator fails or a compliance gap is identified, an <strong>Issue</strong> is 
                        created in the <code>sn_grc_issue</code> table. Issues track the remediation process 
                        from identification through resolution.
                    </p>

                    <h3>Issue Lifecycle</h3>
                    <div class="diagram-block">
                        <div class="diagram-title">Issue Resolution Flow</div>
                        <div class="flow-container">
                            <div class="flow-step">
                                <span class="flow-step-label">New</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-desc">Issue identified from failed indicator</span>
                            </div>
                            <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
                            <div class="flow-step">
                                <span class="flow-step-label">Analyze</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-desc">Root cause analysis and impact assessment</span>
                            </div>
                            <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
                            <div class="flow-step">
                                <span class="flow-step-label">Respond</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-desc">Remediation plan created and assigned</span>
                            </div>
                            <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
                            <div class="flow-step">
                                <span class="flow-step-label">Resolve</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-desc">Fix verified and issue closed</span>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <p>
                        Issues can also be linked to <strong>Risk records</strong> — a persistent compliance gap 
                        may indicate an emerging risk that needs to be formally assessed and treated through the 
                        risk management lifecycle.
                    </p>

                    <div class="info-card info-card--important">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Cross-Module Integration</div>
                        <p>
                            This is where IRM's integrated nature shines. A failed compliance indicator can 
                            automatically generate an issue, which can escalate to a risk, which triggers 
                            a risk response workflow — all without manual handoffs between teams.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </section>

